 ## Background
-Frequency-Modulated Point Cloud Rendering was proposed by Zhang et al. in 2023 [S1]. It addresses the limitations of NeRF-based editing, which often requires pre-trained segmentation models for object-level editing [S1]. The method builds on the idea of Implicit Neural Representations (INRs) and HyperNetworks [S1]. It was evaluated on NeRF-Synthetic, ScanNet, DTU, and Tanks and Temples datasets [S1].
+Frequency-Modulated Point Cloud Rendering was proposed by Zhang et al. in 2023 [S1]. It addresses the limitations of Implicit Neural Representations (INRs) and standard MLPs in point cloud rendering, specifically the spectral bias towards low-frequency signals [S1]. The method builds on the concept of HyperNetworks [11] and Frequency Modulation Networks (MFN) [7] to create an adaptive frequency modulation mechanism. It is part of the broader effort to make neural rendering editable and real-time, competing with methods like CCNeRF and Object-NeRF [S1].
 
